 "title": "Battle of Thermopylae",
 "date_label": "August or September 480 BC",
 "year": -480,
 "intro": "In the summer of 480 BC Xerxes I of Persia led a vast army into Greece to punish Athens and Sparta and to add the mainland to his empire. The one land route south from Thessaly ran through Thermopylae, the Hot Gates, a coastal strip where the cliffs of Mt Kallidromos came close to the Malian Gulf. A small Greek force under the Spartan king Leonidas I blocked it for three days against Persian armies many times its size. The first two days were frontal fights that the defenders won. On the third a local man led a Persian column over the mountains, forcing Leonidas to send most of his army away and make a last stand with the Spartans, Thespians and Thebans. This map replays the fight step by step.",
 "outcome": "Persian victory: the pass was forced and the way into central Greece opened.",
 "uncertainty": "Ancient sources give wildly inflated numbers, and modern estimates of Xerxes' army run from 40,000 to 300,000. Exact dates vary: 21-23 July, 20 August or 8-10 September. The unit sizes follow the standard modern count of about 7,000 Greeks. The position of the Phocian wall, the unit placements, the Persian camp, Hydarnes' route over the mountain and the order of the final retreat are reconstructed and marked conjectural. The shoreline has moved several kilometres; the base map shows the modern coast.",
